title={Carbon and nitrogen storage estimation of tropical seagrass meadows on the eastern coast of Sri Lanka},
source={REGIONAL STUDIES IN MARINE SCIENCE},
proceedings_title={Chinese Academy of Sciences and South China Sea Institute of Oceanology, CAS and Uva Wellassa University and Chinese Academy of Sciences and South China Sea Institute of Oceanology, CAS and Deakin University and Southern Methodist University and Chinese Academy of Sciences and South China Sea Institute of Oceanology, CAS and Shanghai Ocean University and Uva Wellassa University},
volume={90},
article_number={104423},
abstract={Seagrasses have a high blue carbon sequestration capacity in shallow coastal waters. The objectives of this study are to develop accurate maps for three tropical seagrass meadows (Adukkuparu, Salli Beach, and Kalkudah) on the eastern coast of Sri Lanka and to determine their carbon and nitrogen storage capacities. In this study, high-resolution drone images were captured through the flying paths over the seagrass meadows. The Aboveground Biomass (AGB), Belowground Biomass (BGB), and sediment samples were also collected according to the guidelines of the Blue Carbon Manual. The collected drone images were utilized for orthomosaic map generation, further classification, and accuracy assessments. Accordingly, seagrass cover and non-seagrass cover areas were distinguished. In addition, drone observations were combined with in-situ field observations to estimate species-specific seagrass-covered areas. We analyzed AGB, BGB, and sediment carbon and nitrogen stocks using a CHNS elemental analyzer. Results show that the dominant Oceana serrulata and Thalassia hemprichii seagrass species cover an area of 9.35 ha. This area was determined with > 90 % overall accuracy. Both seagrass species stored almost identical amounts of carbon and nitrogen in AGB and BGB. The total carbon and nitrogen stock of the studied seagrass areas are 15.37 ± 0.78 Mg C ha−1 and 0.58 ± 0.09 Mg N ha−1, respectively. Particular total seagrass carbon and nitrogen mass estimations were 52.13 ± 3.74 Mg C and 1.49 ± 0.49 Mg N, respectively. Elevated total organic carbon/total nitrogen ratios (>20) indicated that terrestrial plant-derived organic matter dominates the sampled seagrass sediments. The applied mapping methodology in the current study provides a scalable framework for seagrass assessment, facilitating informed conservation and management strategies. Therefore, this study underscores the necessity for immediate conservation actions and supports efforts toward sustainable marine ecosystem management. © 2025 Elsevier B.V.},
