volume={237},
article_number={110681},
abstract={There is a current trend towards the use of temperate alternative pasture species to either replace or augment perennial ryegrass (PRG) monocultures. Popular alternative temperate species are those that are more water and nutrient use efficient and better able to adapt to future climate scenarios, for example, legumes and herbs. Simultaneously, there has been rapid development of agricultural sensors, including sensor-based technologies for monitoring pasture biomass. However, given the historical dominance of PRG, calibration studies for pasture biomass sensors often use datasets comprising only PRG samples with little consideration of how the sensor might perform on alternative species. This study sought to investigate whether calibration models based on PRG can be applied to monitor alternative pasture species swards accurately and, should recalibration be required, how to recalibrate most efficiently. The alternative species investigated were cocksfoot, chicory, red clover, and a three-species mixture of those same species. Two recalibration options were investigated: either developing sward-specific models for each sward type investigated or broad-based models that combined calibration data across multiple swards. An experimental area was established where each of these swards, plus PRG for comparison, were sown in adjacent 1 ha plots. Plots were subject to rotational grazing with regrowth lengths altered to suit each species type and were surveyed fortnightly for biomass. Reference datasets were collected over 12 months aligned with sensor data from four different sensors or tools including UAV-borne multispectral sensor (UAV), hyperspectral camera (HYP), ultrasonic sensor (SON), and rising plate meter (RPM). A fifth reference dataset for a satellite-borne multispectral sensor (SAT) was gathered from the same location over an extended 18-month period. Sward-specific biomass models were built for each sward and sensor, as well as broad-based biomass models, including data from all swards per sensor. Independent validation results showed that predictive performance was reduced for spectral imaging sensors UAV, HYP and SAT when applying a PRG-based model to an alternative species without recalibration. However, PRG-based models for active sensors, SON and RPM, could be applied to other species without penalty due to their reliance on canopy height which proved to be a strong and stable predictor of biomass across pasture types. Recalibration via either method improved prediction performance in UAV, HYP and SAT, whereas no additional increase in accuracy was achieved through recalibration for SON and RPM. Lin's Concordance Correlation Coefficients (LCCC) for sward-specific models ranged from 0.49 − 0.88 across swards and sensors with an overall average of 0.71. Broad-based model LCCC ranged from 0.36 − 0.88 across swards and sensors, with an overall average of 0.72. It was concluded that applying models based on PRG to other species where the sensor relies or partially relies on spectral features as model drivers is not recommended. Instead, for those sensors, recalibration should occur using a broad-based or sward-specific modelling approach dependent on the species and sensor. © 2025},
