volume={239},
article_number={111086},
abstract={The spread of noxious weeds in alpine meadows threatens grassland productivity and biodiversity. Accurate identification is challenging because both weeds and native grasses are low-growing and vegetation is highly heterogeneous. High-resolution UAV hyperspectral imagery is a valuable data source for fine-scale ecological monitoring. To accurately identify noxious weeds in alpine meadows, this study extracted spectral, textural, and geometric features, and applied Mahalanobis distance with RFECV to select key spectral bands, constructing an optimized multi-feature dataset. The performance of multiple machine learning and deep learning models, including Random Forest and 3D-CNN, is systematically compared to achieve fine-scale and high-accuracy identification. The results show that: (1) Noxious weeds (Elsholtzia densa Benth and Aconitum gymnandrum Maxim) exhibit significant differences in the first and second derivatives within the 650–800 nm wavelength range, as well as in the 550 nm region of the derivative envelope spectra; (2) In the multi-feature variable set constructed from the sensitive bands selected based on RFECV and Mahalanobis distance, spectral indices contribute 64.98% (with NGNDVI, RENDVI, and SR being the most significant), while MNF and continuum removal contribute 12.27% and 8.72%, respectively; (3) Among nine identification models, the 3D-CNN model achieved the best overall recognition performance for noxious weeds, with an overall accuracy (OA) of up to 99.55% and a Kappa coefficient of 0.9929. This study demonstrates the potential of multi-feature fusion combined with deep learning for fine-grained identification of noxious weeds, providing high-precision technical support for ecological restoration of alpine meadows. © 2025 Elsevier B.V.},
