address={Natl & Kapodistrian Univ Athens, Fac Geol & Geoenvironm, Sch Sci, Dept Dynam Tecton Appl Geol, Athens 15784, Greece and Natl & Kapodistrian Univ Athens, Fac Geol & Geoenvironm, Sch Sci, Dept Geog & Climatol, Athens 15784, Greece and Harokopio Univ, Dept Geog, Kallithea 17676, Greece},
year={2026},
title={UAS-Based Photogrammetric Assessment of Geomorphological Changes Along the Lilas River (Evia Island, Central Greece) After the August 2020 Flood},
source={Applied Sciences (Switzerland)},
proceedings_title={National & Kapodistrian University of Athens and National & Kapodistrian University of Athens and Harokopio University Athens},
volume={16},
article_number={1456},
abstract={Geomorphological change is a fundamental consequence of high-magnitude flood events, as extreme hydraulic forcing can rapidly reshape river channels, redistribute sediment, and alter floodplain connectivity. This study applies multi-temporal UAS-based Structure-from-Motion (SfM) photogrammetry to quantify flood-induced geomorphological changes along two representative reaches of the Lilas River (Evia Island, Central Greece) affected by the extreme August 2020 flash flood. High-resolution aerial surveys were conducted prior to the event (June 2018) and shortly after the flood (September 2020), producing Digital Surface Models (DSMs) and orthomosaics with a ground sampling distance of ~2.5 cm. Differential DSM analysis reveals pronounced spatial heterogeneity in erosion and deposition, with net erosional lowering locally exceeding 7 m and depositional aggradation reaching up to ~5 m after accounting for vegetation effects. Channel widening was the dominant response, with cross-sectional widths increasing by a factor of three to nine at selected locations, driven primarily by lateral bank erosion. The results highlight the strong interaction between extreme hydrological forcing, loose alluvial sediments, vegetation removal, and human interventions such as roads and engineered terraces. The study demonstrates how repeatable UAS–SfM workflows can provide quantitative evidence to support post-flood assessment, guide infrastructure adaptation, and inform river restoration and flood risk management in Mediterranean catchments prone to extreme events. © 2026 by the authors.},
keywords={flash flood; flood impact; geomorphological changes; GIS; high-resolution mapping; photogrammetry; Structure-from-Motion; UAS; UAV; Antennas; Bank protection; Catchments; Deposition; Erosion; Flood control; Floods; Geographic information systems; Geomorphology; Landforms; Risk assessment; Risk management; Rivers; Unmanned aerial vehicles (UAV); Vegetation; Digital surface models; Flash-floods; Flood event; Flood impact; Forcings; Geomorphological change; High-resolution mapping; River channels; Structure from motion; UAS; Photogrammetry; Article; documentation; flooding; geometry; geomorphology; image analysis; image processing; photogrammetry; risk management; river; sediment; shear stress; unmanned aerial vehicle; vegetation; volumetry},
